  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curves $y=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-1$ and $y=2-|x|.$ If $R=[-2,2]\\times [-1,2],$ and\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y), & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin D\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y), & (x,y)\\in R \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin R\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y), & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$",
  "worked_solution": "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14114537/assets/1a5369b58b14444c5ea0ce4bb36954d1d0b7964dcef4e95f9cc527694b3a9e4f.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- Functions I and III equal $x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)$ on $D$ and equal $0$ outside $D.$\n\n- We cannot use function II. The function is not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(1,2)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the function is nonzero at this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
